---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { foods } from '../../data/foods';

const title = "Food Calorie Database | Track Macros & Calories per 100g";
const description = "Explore our comprehensive database of foods. Find out exactly how many calories, protein, carbs, and fats are in 100g of your favorite foods.";

// Group foods by category for a better directory layout
const foodsByCategory = foods.reduce((acc, food) => {
  if (!acc[food.category]) {
    acc[food.category] = [];
  }
  acc[food.category].push(food);
  return acc;
}, {} as Record<string, typeof foods>);

const categories = Object.keys(foodsByCategory).sort();
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/foods/">
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    
    <div class="text-center mb-16">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight mb-4">
        The Ultimate <span class="text-brand">Food Database</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-slate-600 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        Search our directory to find exactly how many calories and macronutrients are in 100g of your favorite foods.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mb-16">
      <h2>The Ultimate Guide to Food Calories, Nutrition, and Macronutrients</h2>
      <p>
        Navigating the complex world of nutrition can often feel overwhelming. With countless diets, varying nutritional advice, and misleading food labels, finding a reliable and scientific approach to what you eat is more important than ever. This guide is designed to demystify food calories and macronutrients, giving you the knowledge you need to make informed decisions about your diet.
      </p>
      
      <h3>Why Do We Measure Food in 100g Increments?</h3>
      <p>
        You might have noticed that nutritional information worldwide—including in our food database—is standardized to 100-gram portions. But why is this specific measurement universally adopted?
      </p>
      <p>
        The primary reason is <strong>standardization and comparability</strong>. When you look at a food label that lists nutritional facts based on an arbitrary "serving size," it can be incredibly difficult to compare two different products. For example, one brand of cereal might define a serving size as 30 grams, while another might define it as 55 grams. If you're trying to figure out which cereal is higher in sugar or protein, you have to do mental gymnastics to equate the two.
      </p>
      <p>
        By measuring everything in 100-gram increments, the playing field is completely leveled. A 100g measurement essentially translates to a percentage. If a food has 15g of sugar per 100g, you immediately know that the food is 15% sugar by weight. This makes it instantly obvious how dense a food is in various nutrients or calories, allowing you to compare an apple directly to a slice of bread without worrying about the size or weight of individual servings.
      </p>
      <p>
        Additionally, the 100g metric system aligns with global scientific standards. Nutritionists, dietitians, and food scientists use grams to measure the mass of food accurately. When creating meal plans, using a kitchen scale and measuring your food in grams provides the highest level of accuracy for calorie counting and macro tracking, leading to much more reliable health outcomes than estimating based on volume (like cups or tablespoons) which can vary wildly depending on how a food is chopped or packed.
      </p>

      <h3>What Are Macronutrients?</h3>
      <p>
        Macronutrients, often colloquially referred to as "macros," are the primary building blocks of the food we consume. They are required by the body in large ("macro") amounts to sustain life, provide energy, and facilitate complex bodily functions. There are three main macronutrients: carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. Alcohol is sometimes considered a fourth macronutrient because it provides calories, but it is not essential for survival.
      </p>
      
      <h4>Carbohydrates (4 calories per gram)</h4>
      <p>
        Carbohydrates are the body's preferred and most efficient source of energy. When you eat carbs, your digestive system breaks them down into glucose (blood sugar), which is transported to your cells and used for fuel. The brain and central nervous system rely heavily on glucose to function optimally. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Carbohydrates are generally categorized into two types: simple and complex. Simple carbs (like sugar, honey, and fruit juice) digest rapidly, providing a quick burst of energy. Complex carbs (like oats, brown rice, and starchy vegetables) digest slowly because of their fiber content, providing a steady release of energy and helping you feel fuller for longer. Fiber is a crucial type of carbohydrate that the body cannot fully digest, playing an essential role in gut health and regulating blood sugar spikes.
      </p>

      <h4>Proteins (4 calories per gram)</h4>
      <p>
        Protein is the structural foundation of the human body. It is vital for building and repairing tissues, including muscle, skin, hair, and nails. Proteins are also necessary for producing enzymes and hormones, and for supporting a robust immune system. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Proteins are made up of smaller molecules called amino acids. While the body can synthesize many amino acids, there are nine "essential" amino acids that must be obtained through your diet. Animal-based proteins (meat, dairy, eggs) are considered "complete" proteins because they contain all nine essential amino acids in adequate amounts. Many plant-based proteins (beans, lentils, nuts) are "incomplete," but combining different plant proteins throughout the day can easily fulfill your essential amino acid requirements. Protein is also highly satiating, meaning it helps you feel full and satisfied after a meal.
      </p>

      <h4>Fats (9 calories per gram)</h4>
      <p>
        Fats have historically been demonized in diet culture, but they are absolutely essential for a healthy body. Fats are incredibly energy-dense, providing more than double the calories per gram compared to carbs and protein. They serve as an energy reserve, protect vital organs, provide insulation, and are crucial for the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K). 
      </p>
      <p>
        The quality of fat matters significantly. Unsaturated fats (found in olive oil, avocados, nuts, and fatty fish) are generally heart-healthy and can improve blood cholesterol levels. Saturated fats (found mainly in animal products like butter, cheese, and red meat, as well as coconut oil) should be consumed in moderation. Trans fats, often found in heavily processed foods, are harmful to cardiovascular health and should be avoided as much as possible.
      </p>

      <h3>How to Read Nutritional Data</h3>
      <p>
        Understanding how to decipher a nutrition facts label or database entry is a superpower for taking control of your health. When you look at our food directory, here is how you should interpret the data:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>1. Check the Calories:</strong> This tells you the total energy provided by 100g of the food. If your goal is weight loss, you generally need to consume fewer calories than you burn (a caloric deficit). If your goal is muscle gain, you need a caloric surplus. Understanding the calorie density of a food is the first step in managing your total daily energy intake.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>2. Analyze the Macronutrient Breakdown:</strong> Look at how those calories are distributed among protein, carbs, and fats. A highly processed snack might have 500 calories per 100g, consisting almost entirely of sugar (carbs) and unhealthy fats. Conversely, 100g of chicken breast will have around 165 calories, consisting almost entirely of high-quality protein with zero carbs and minimal fat. Your macro split should align with your specific health and fitness goals.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>3. Look Beyond the Macros:</strong> While our directory focuses primarily on calories and macronutrients, remember that micronutrients (vitamins and minerals) are equally important for overall health. A diet consisting entirely of protein powder and refined sugar might hit your macronutrient targets, but it will leave you severely deficient in essential vitamins, minerals, and fiber.
      </p>

      <h3>The Science of Energy Density</h3>
      <p>
        Energy density is a fundamental concept in nutrition and weight management. It refers to the number of calories (energy) present in a specific weight of food. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Foods with <strong>high energy density</strong> pack a lot of calories into a small volume or weight. Think of peanut butter, olive oil, nuts, and chocolate. Because they have very little water and a high fat content (remember, fat is 9 calories per gram), you consume a significant number of calories in just a few bites. While many high-energy-density foods are healthy (like nuts and avocados), it is very easy to accidentally overeat them if you aren't paying attention to portion sizes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Foods with <strong>low energy density</strong> provide fewer calories for the same weight of food. These are typically foods that have high water and fiber contents, such as fruits, vegetables, and clear soups. For example, 100g of spinach or watermelon contains a very small number of calories because a large percentage of that weight is simply water. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Understanding energy density allows you to practice <em>volume eating</em>. Volume eating is a dietary strategy where you prioritize low-energy-density foods, allowing you to eat a physically large, satisfying volume of food while keeping your total calorie intake relatively low. This is an incredibly effective strategy for weight loss or maintenance because it physically fills your stomach, triggering stretch receptors that signal fullness to your brain, without breaking your calorie budget. By strategically combining low-energy-density foods (like vegetables) with moderate amounts of higher-energy-density proteins and healthy fats, you can build meals that are both highly satiating and nutritionally balanced.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      {categories.map((category) => (
        <section>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-8 border-b pb-4">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">{category}</h2>
            <span class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-600 text-sm font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full">
              {foodsByCategory[category].length} items
            </span>
          </div>
          
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
            {foodsByCategory[category].map((food) => (
              <a href={`/foods/${food.slug}/`} class="block group h-full">
                <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm hover:shadow-lg transition-all group-hover:-translate-y-1 h-full flex flex-col justify-between">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 group-hover:text-brand transition-colors">{food.name}</h3>
                    <p class="text-slate-500 text-sm line-clamp-2 mb-4">{food.description}</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4 border-t border-slate-100">
                    <span class="font-black text-slate-800 text-lg">{food.calories} <span class="text-sm text-slate-400 font-normal">kcal</span></span>
                    <span class="text-sm font-medium text-brand bg-canvas-soft px-2 py-1 rounded-md">{food.protein}g Protein</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mt-16 border-t pt-16">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
      
      <h4>How accurate are the calorie counts on this site?</h4>
      <p>
        The nutritional data provided in our database is highly accurate and sourced from established scientific and agricultural databases. However, it is important to remember that all calorie counts—even those on food labels—are averages. Natural variations in growing conditions, soil quality, ripeness, and preparation methods mean that the exact caloric content of an individual piece of fruit or cut of meat may vary slightly from the recorded average. Use these numbers as a reliable guide rather than an absolute certainty.
      </p>
      
      <h4>Should I weigh my food raw or cooked?</h4>
      <p>
        For the highest degree of accuracy, you should always weigh your food in its raw, uncooked state. Cooking changes the weight of food significantly by either removing water (e.g., cooking meat makes it lighter) or absorbing water (e.g., boiling rice or pasta makes it heavier). A 100g piece of raw chicken breast will weigh less after it is grilled, but the total calories and protein remain the same. The nutritional values in our database typically reflect the raw state unless specifically noted otherwise.
      </p>

      <h4>Are all calories created equal?</h4>
      <p>
        From a pure thermodynamic perspective of weight loss or weight gain, a calorie is a calorie. If you consume more calories than you burn, you will gain weight, regardless of whether those calories came from broccoli or ice cream. However, from a health, hormonal, and satiety perspective, all calories are definitely not equal. 100 calories of lean protein will keep you full for hours, support muscle growth, and require more energy for your body to digest (the thermic effect of food). 100 calories of refined sugar will cause a blood sugar spike, leave you feeling hungry shortly after, and provide no beneficial nutrients.
      </p>

      <h4>Is a low-carb or low-fat diet better?</h4>
      <p>
        There is no single "best" diet that works for everyone. Both low-carb (such as Keto) and low-fat diets can be highly effective for weight loss as long as you maintain a caloric deficit. The best diet for you is the one that you can adhere to consistently in the long term, that makes you feel energized, and that fits seamlessly into your lifestyle and food preferences. Focusing on high-quality whole foods, adequate protein intake, and a healthy balance of macros is generally the most sustainable approach.
      </p>

      <h4>What is the Thermic Effect of Food (TEF)?</h4>
      <p>
        The Thermic Effect of Food (TEF) refers to the energy (calories) your body expends to digest, absorb, and metabolize the food you eat. Different macronutrients have different thermic effects. Protein has the highest TEF, meaning your body burns up to 20-30% of the calories from protein just to digest it. Carbohydrates have a TEF of around 5-10%, and fats have a TEF of around 0-3%. This is another reason why a high-protein diet is often recommended for weight management.
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>
</Layout>
